Changes requested: 1 in-scope test failure (regex bug), 1 silent bug in same pattern.

**Failing test:** `parseSkillsFromBody > extracts skill names from ## Skills section` — returns `["beads-workflow"]` instead of `["beads-workflow", "stack-typescript"]`.

**Root cause:** The regex on line 34 of `src/tools/scaffold.ts`:
`/^## Skills\s*\n([\s\S]*?)(?=\n## |\n*$)/m`

The `\n*$` alternative in the lookahead, combined with the `m` (multiline) flag, causes `$` to match at the end of ANY line — not just end-of-string. The lazy `[\s\S]*?` therefore stops after capturing just `- beads-workflow` (the first entry), because the position before its trailing `\n` satisfies `\n*$` (zero newlines + end-of-line).

**Same bug in `injectSkillsIntoBody`** (line 59): identical regex used in `.replace()`. The match is effectively just the `## Skills\n` header (empty capture), so the replacement leaves old skill entries in the body. Current test passes only because it uses weak `toContain` assertions on a single-entry section.

**Fix (both functions):** Replace the fragile regex with a line-by-line approach that is not affected by multiline anchoring:
```typescript
// parseSkillsFromBody
const lines = body.split('\n');
const idx = lines.findIndex(l => /^## Skills\s*$/.test(l));
if (idx === -1) return [];
for (let i = idx + 1; i < lines.length; i++) {
  if (lines[i].startsWith('## ')) break;
  // ... parse `- skill` entries
}
```
For `injectSkillsIntoBody`, use the same line-index approach to find the section boundaries, then splice the lines array.

**Checks run:**
- `npm run typecheck` → 0 errors ✅
- `npm run build` → success ✅
- `bun test tests/detect-stack.test.ts` → 21 pass, 1 fail ❌
- `bun test` (full suite) → 149 pass, 6 fail (5 pre-existing from other beads: 3 in br.test.ts, 2 in lint.test.ts)

**Out-of-scope failures (pre-existing):** br.test.ts (3 failures from opencode-2c6.2/2c6.3), lint.test.ts (2 failures from opencode-2c6.5)

Changes requested: 2 in-scope test failures — same multiline regex bug class that was fixed in opencode-2c6.4.

**Root cause:** The `extractSection` function (src/lib/lint.ts line 43-50) uses `(?=\n## |$)` with the `m` (multiline) flag. With `m`, `$` matches at the end of ANY line, not just end-of-string. The lazy `[\s\S]*?` therefore captures only up to the first line boundary — not the full section.

**Failing test 1:** `fails when ## Context is empty` — After `.replace('Build a new widget...', '')`, the content between `## Context` and `## Skills` is just whitespace. The greedy `\s*\n` in the regex prefix eats all whitespace, and the lazy capture group then grabs `## Skills` itself (stopping at the `\n` after 'Skills' where `$` matches). So `extractSection(body, 'Context')` returns `'## Skills'` — non-empty, non-placeholder — and the linter incorrectly says Context is fine.

**Failing test 2:** `fails when skill is not in known skills registry` — `extractSection(body, 'Skills')` captures only `- beads-workflow` (the first line) because `$` matches at the end of that line. The second line `- stack-nonexistent` is never seen, so no unknown-skill error is generated.

**Fix:** Replace `extractSection` with the same line-by-line scanning pattern that was applied to `parseSkillsFromBody` / `injectSkillsIntoBody` in bead opencode-2c6.4:

```typescript
function extractSection(body: string, sectionName: string): string | null {
  const lines = body.split('\n');
  const header = `## ${sectionName}`;
  const idx = lines.findIndex(l => l.trimEnd() === header || l.trimEnd().startsWith(header + ' '));
  if (idx === -1) return null;
  const contentLines: string[] = [];
  for (let i = idx + 1; i < lines.length; i++) {
    if (lines[i].startsWith('## ')) break;
    contentLines.push(lines[i]);
  }
  return contentLines.join('\n');
}
```

This eliminates the regex entirely and is immune to multiline `$` anchor edge cases.

**Checks run:**
- `npm run typecheck` → 0 errors ✅
- `npm run build` → success ✅
- `bun test tests/lint.test.ts` → 20 pass, 2 fail ❌ (both in-scope)
